freehire used to have semantic search, and then we took it out. It is back now,
for technical postings.

Semantic search ranks a posting by what it means rather than by the words it
uses. Someone who wants to "keep payment systems secure" finds security roles at
payment companies, even when the posting never says it that way. To do that,
every posting needs an embedding: a list of numbers that describes its meaning.

## Why it went away

Two problems, one after the other.

First, it was slow and expensive. Computing an embedding for every posting in a
catalogue this size took a long time and cost more than the feature was worth.

Once we got that under control, the second problem showed up: the index of
embeddings no longer fit on the server's disk.

## What changed

We moved to a new server with room for the index. And embeddings no longer come
from our machines alone: the [volunteer client](/semantic-search) lets anyone
lend their computer for a while, run the embedding model locally, and send the
results back.

## What it does not cover yet

Only technical postings have embeddings for now. Postings outside IT are still
found by keywords alone. The [semantic search page](/semantic-search) shows,
live, how much of the catalogue is covered and how fast the rest is going.

## Where to use it

- On a job page, **Similar jobs** finds postings that describe the same work in
  different words.
- AI agents can ask for it directly: set `semantic` to `true` on a search
  through the MCP server, the `freehire` command-line tool or the agent API. The
  response says whether the semantic ranking actually ran. See
  [For AI agents](/agents) to connect one.
